The Nifty 50 needs to see consistent follow-up buying interest, although the index has witnessed a gradual recovery over the past three consecutive sessions, as it is not out of the woods yet. Caution still prevails given the weakness in the broader structure, although momentum indicators suggest that bearish strength is weakening. As long as the index holds the 23,100-23,000 support zone, a gradual upmove towards 23,500-23,600 is possible. However, a break below this zone could increase the chances of panic selling. Meanwhile, the Bank Nifty needs to defend 56,000 for an upmove towards 56,800-57,000. A break below 56,000 could open the way for a decline towards 55,700-55,600, according to experts.
On September 18, the Nifty 50 rose 76 points, or 0.33 percent, to 23,346, while the Bank Nifty rallied 303 points, or 0.54 percent, to 56,359. Market breadth remained positive, with about 2,151 shares advancing against 1,094 declining shares on the National Stock Exchange.
 Jatin Gedia, VP - Technical Research at Teji Mandi Investment Technologies 
The Nifty continued its decline for the sixth consecutive week. It tested the previous swing low placed in the 23,200-23,000 zone and halted its decline. During the entire decline, the 40-hourly moving average has acted as a resistance, and pullbacks towards this moving average have been sold into. The moving average is currently placed at 23,350. Thus, it becomes important for the Nifty to sustain above 23,350 for a reversal of the current downtrend.
If the Nifty manages to sustain above 23,350, we can expect a rally towards 23,750 and 23,950, which are the 38.2 percent and 50 percent Fibonacci retracement levels of the entire decline from 24,774 towards 23,100. In case the Nifty decisively breaches 23,000, we can expect it to drift towards 22,500-22,600, where support in the form of a rising trendline, derived by joining the previous couple of swing lows formed in April 2025 (21,743) and April 2026 (22,182), is placed.
On the derivatives front, the maximum Put open interest is placed at 23,300, followed by the 23,000 strike, while the maximum Call open interest is placed at 24,000, followed by the 23,700 strike. This implies a range of 23,300-23,700. The Put-Call ratio improved from 0.97 to 1.13, indicating slightly bullish positioning. Thus, considering the technical and derivatives data points, we expect the Nifty to consolidate between 23,300 and 23,650.
 Strategy : Buy Nifty September Futures at around 23,378, with a stop-loss of 23,250 and a target of 23,580.
 Hitesh Rathi, Technical Analyst (Equity & Derivatives) at Angel One 
Prices hovering close to crucial swing supports, coupled with oversold readings across market breadth and technical indicators, have helped the Nifty 50 stage a bounce over the past few days. A quick glance at the weekly charts also indicates a clear rejection from the 23,100-23,000 band, pointing towards the presence of strong demand at lower levels.
However, this recovery has had little impact on the overall technical structure, with the setup continuing to remain cautious. It would therefore be safe to say that the index is not yet out of the woods. As long as the index holds above the aforementioned support band, prices could continue to inch higher, albeit at a grudging pace, given the presence of strong supply at higher levels.
Since prices have now retraced more than 50 percent of the down bar witnessed earlier this week, while the broader trend continues to remain aligned to the downside, the ongoing secondary retracement could extend towards the 23,500-23,600 band. In such a scenario, we advocate adopting a strategy where dips could be bought into, while rallies towards key resistance zones can be utilised to consider short positions.
In terms of levels, while the 23,100-23,000 band remains a sacrosanct support level, immediate support can be found in the 23,250-23,200 zone. On the flip side, immediate resistance is likely to be encountered in the 23,450-23,500 band, while the 23,600 level, which was a prior support, is now likely to act as resistance, following the principle of polarity.
 Strategy : Sell Nifty Futures near 23,400, with a stop-loss of 23,480 and book profits near 23,300-23,250.
 Jatin Gedia, VP - Technical Research at Teji Mandi Investment Technologies 
Bank Nifty continued to consolidate within the previous week's range of 57,430-55,700 and thereby formed an inside-bar pattern on the weekly charts. The consolidation was on the back of divergence between the two key heavyweights of the Bank Nifty. The gains in HDFC Bank were offset by the decline in ICICI Bank, thereby keeping the Bank Nifty range-bound.
The formation of an inside-bar pattern makes the extremes of the range, i.e. 57,430 and 55,700, crucial levels to watch out for during the week. A breach of this range on either side could lead to a directional trend. Support is placed in the 54,800-54,700 zone, coinciding with the lower end of the reverse channel, while immediate resistance is placed in the 57,000-57,200 zone, as per the role-reversal principle.
 Strategy : Buy Bank Nifty September Futures at around 56,527, with a stop-loss of 56,350 and a target of 57,000-57,200.
 Hitesh R

